## Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)

**Q1: Can non-residents get a Virginia CCW permit?**

Yes, if you hold a valid concealed-carry permit from a state Virginia recognizes, you can apply for reciprocity. However, you can't get a Virginia-issued permit unless you are a resident.

**Q2: How long is a Virginia concealed carry permit good for?**

The permit is valid for five years from its issue date. Renewing requires a new background check but no extra training, unless state regulations change.

**Q3: What's the difference between a standard and an express CCW permit class?**

A standard class usually takes two days (or a full weekend) and offers more hands-on practice. An express class condenses the curriculum into a single day, which is great for those with limited time.

**Q4: Do I need to own a firearm before taking the course?**

No, you can use a loaner firearm provided by the training facility for the live-fire portion. However, you must legally own a firearm when you apply for the permit.

**Q5: Are there any extra fees beyond the $80 application fee?**

Some training providers charge separate course fees, typically ranging from $100-$250. There might also be a small fee for background-check processing if the state changes its fee structure.

**Q6: Can I carry my concealed weapon in other states?**

Virginia has reciprocity agreements with many states. Always check the latest reciprocity map before traveling, as laws can change without notice.

**Q7: What if I don't pass the written exam?**

You can retake the exam after a short waiting period (usually 24-48 hours). Most instructors allow unlimited attempts during the same course.

**Q8: Is ongoing training required to keep my permit valid?**

No, Virginia doesn't require continuous training for renewal, but it's highly recommended for maintaining safety and proficiency.

